Q#3: The difference between the Stack and the Heap in C#

In C#, the Stack and the Heap are two different areas of memory used for storing data.
The Stack is a region of memory that is used for storing value types (such as int, float, and structs)
and reference types such as classes and structs that are created on the stack. 
The Stack is organized in a last-in, first-out (LIFO) manner,
meaning that the most recently added item is the first one to be removed."اخر حاجة دخلت هي أول حاجة تخرج".
The Stack is also faster than the Heap because it has a fixed size and does not require garbage collection.

The Heap, on the other hand, is a region of memory that is used for storing reference types 
such as objects, arrays, strings, and class instances that are created on the heap.
The Heap is organized in a more complex manner than the Stack, and it can grow and shrink dynamically as needed.
But it is slower than the Stack because it requires garbage collection to manage memory,
and does not require to remove items in a specific order like the Stack.
